    <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
    <h1>Why I built Mexico Brief</h1>
    <p class="thesis">I’m Alan Lacroix. I was born in Mexico City, grew up in Miami, and now I’m building a business in Mexico.</p>
    <p>When I started spending more time there, I realized I understood the country less than I thought. The peso was at 17.5, but I couldn’t explain whether that was good or bad. The minimum wage had tripled in three years, but I didn’t know why—or what had changed because of it.</p>
    <p>I wanted somewhere I could start with the facts and come to my own conclusions.</p>
    <p>Most of those facts already exist. Banxico, INEGI, Hacienda, and other public institutions publish an enormous amount of information. But it is scattered across websites, spreadsheets, reports, and press releases. News coverage creates a different problem: plenty of urgency, but often too little context—and an image of Mexico dominated by cartel violence.</p>
    <p>Mexico Brief brings the numbers and the news together. It follows the official data, selects the developments worth paying attention to, explains the background, and separates what happened from what I think it means.</p>
    <p>Every figure includes its source and period. When I give you my view, I label it. You can agree, disagree, or ignore it and use the underlying information to reach your own conclusion.</p>
    <p>I originally built the site for myself. Then family and friends started using it, so I made it public.</p>
    <p>I look at Mexico partly from a US vantage point and partly as someone trying to build there. I’m interested in its political stability, economic growth, relationship with the United States, and position in technology, AI, private equity, payments, and entrepreneurship.</p>
    <p>I don’t expect the numbers to settle every argument. I want them to make the argument better—and make Mexico easier to understand without reducing it to the loudest headline.</p>
    <p>Mexico is one of the world’s largest economies, central to the United States’ economic future, and still somehow a dependable first-knockout-round exit in the World Cup.</p>
